{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Bone Garden: Sulfur Bacteria of a Baltic Shipwreck",
    "date": "2026-11-11",
    "lesson": "A wooden shipwreck on the anoxic Baltic seafloor is a slow-motion whale fall made of oak and tar instead of blubber. Shipworms are absent here (too brackish, too cold), so the timbers rot by microbial action alone: fermenters and sulfate-reducers work the waterlogged wood in the anoxic mud pocket beneath the hull, producing sulfide that feeds sulfide-oxidizing bacteria on the exposed, oxygenated topside. The two populations never trade places - they are pinned to opposite faces of the same plank by whether oxygen reaches them. Meanwhile the cold (4-6 C), high-latitude darkness and near-zero currents of the Baltic deep basins are exactly why 17th-century wrecks here are so well preserved: almost nothing eats the wood quickly. Play as a sulfur-oxidizer founder colonizing the oxic rim of a sunken hull timber, racing to build biomass off the sulfide seeping up through the wood before cold, dark, and the drifting grazers above shut you down.",
    "citation": "Baltic Sea shipwreck microbiology (Vrouw Maria-type wrecks); wood-fall sulfidic microbial succession studies",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Anoxic Baltic Sea shipwrecks (e.g. 17th-18th c. wooden hulls in the Gulf of Finland/Bothnia) preserved by cold, dark, brackish, low-oxygen deep basins; sulfate-reducing and sulfide-oxidizing bacterial succession on waterlogged wood"
  },
